// ==================================================================
//  A SOCIETY OF PIECE-AGENTS THAT PLAYS CHESS
// ==================================================================
//
//  Companion listing to "Sixteen Scientists on One Board". Every construction
//  of the note appears here as ordinary code:
//
//    Sec. 5    the board        -- Board, Cells, Settle, Ray  (three families)
//    Sec. 5.3  legality         -- Legal, Pins                (the world's ALL)
//    Sec. 6    the ply is a join-- Ply                        (N-ary, atomic)
//    Sec. 7    coordination     -- Franchise, Advocate, Gate  (quorum = arity)
//    Sec. 7.6  rounds           -- Clock                      (+ last resort)
//    Sec. 8    coalition rung   -- Ladder rung 6              (//! ideal)
//    Sec. 9    material         -- Harvest, Live              (conservation)
//
//  CONVENTION. A parameter written `c` is a NAME (a channel); a parameter
//  written `@d` is DATA. To pass a channel one sends `*c`, since @(*c) = c.
//
//  NOTATION. Guards are written in the generated spatial-behavioural logic
//  extended with the coalition modality: out(n)phi, in(n)phi, phi | psi, the
//  name predicate @phi, the context-labelled modality <K_m>phi, its settled
//  form <<K_m>>phi, the greatest fixed point nu X. phi, graded separation
//  phi |_k^N over a DESCRIBED namespace N, and <E>_N^V psi over opens of N.
//  There is no hidden-name quantifier and no need of one: `new` is syntactic
//  sugar, since a name is a quoted process and no process contains the name
//  that codes it, so a name fresh in P is manufactured by quoting any term
//  properly containing P. Every family below is manufactured that way, which
//  is what makes it DESCRIBABLE and hence what makes |_k^N and <E>_N^V apply.
//  The current reducer accepts only the arithmetic and pattern fragment of
//  `where`; guards using a structural, modal or coalition connective are
//  marked `//! ideal` and specify a check rather than perform one.
//
//  THE THREE FAMILIES (Sec. 5.1). Square s of board b carries:
//      occ(s) = @[*b,"occ",s]        occupancy; the ONLY family a ply consumes
//      atk(s,c,p) = @[*b,"atk",s,c,p]  deposited attack markers; read-only
//      pin(s) / chk(c) / ep(s) / cast(c,side)   deposited constraints
//  Were attack markers deposited on occ, a scan signalling "p bears on s"
//  could rendezvous with a move protocol receiving on occ(s) and the scan
//  would PLAY A PLY. Perception must not be able to act; the separation of
//  access grades one and two is enforced here by a choice of namespace, and
//  with distinct families the failure is UNREPRESENTABLE rather than merely
//  avoided by discipline.
//
//  CONSERVATION. Nothing mints. Franchise issues one LINEAR advocacy token per
//  live piece per round and does not carry it over. Harvest MOVES a captured
//  agent's stack into the capturer's. Every Live debit moves a token from
//  sigma into kappa. So sigma + kappa = Theta throughout.
// ==================================================================

new stdout(`rho:io:stdout`), display, arena, wall, player, clock,
    Live, Wall, Board, Cells, Ray, Settle, Pins, Legal, Ply, Harvest,
    Registry, Franchise, Piece, Candidates, Evaluate, Advocate, Gate,
    Ladder, Revise, Check, Turn, Round, Adjudicate, Boot
in {

  for (@line <= display) { stdout!(line) }

  // ==================================================================
  //  METABOLISM  --  reclaim, debit, reissue.
  //  Between the receipt and the send the stack is a message in flight: living
  //  is racing others to your own stack, and the metabolic channel m is
  //  exactly the vulnerability that Harvest exploits when a piece is captured.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Live(m, @burn, step) = {
      for (@sigma <- m) {
        match sigma > burn {
          true  => { m!(sigma - burn) | *step | Live!(*m, burn, *step) }
          false => { display!(("DIED", "could not fund a step")) }
        }
      }
    }

  // A source, not a prey: rate-limited, non-exclusive, non-adaptive, finite.
  | contract Wall(w, @quantum, @reserve) = {
      for (ret <- w) {
        match reserve >= quantum {
          true  => { ret!(quantum) | Wall!(*w, quantum, reserve - quantum) }
          false => { ret!(0) | Wall!(*w, quantum, 0) }
        }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE BOARD  --  occupancy only. Note what is NOT here: a `new` for the
  //  squares. Each square name is the quote of a term properly containing the
  //  board, so by the no-self-code theorem it cannot already occur in the
  //  board -- locally fresh, no registry consulted, decentralised by
  //  construction. And because the family is generated by a predicate on
  //  names, the whole namespace can be DESCRIBED rather than enumerated.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Board(b, @placement) = { Cells!(*b, placement, 0) | Settle!(*b) }

  | contract Cells(b, @placement, @s) = {
      match s < 64 {
        true  => {   @[*b,"occ",s]!(placement.nth(s))
                   | Cells!(*b, placement, s + 1) }
        false => { Nil }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE SETTLING  --  Sec. 5.2 and Rem. 5.3.
  //
  //  A ray walk PEEKS at occupancy (`<<-`) and never consumes it. That is what
  //  makes the scan perception rather than interaction: with a consuming
  //  receipt the settling would move the pieces by looking at them, and
  //  sixteen agents scanning concurrently would serialise on every square.
  //
  //  Termination: each step consumes one square of a strictly decreasing
  //  remaining ray. Confluence: markers are deposited on the atk family, which
  //  nothing in the settling reads. That discharges the obligation <<K_m>>
  //  depends on, which in noughts was Lem. 8 and here is harder because the
  //  walk is recursive in occupancy.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Ray(b, @from, @dir, @p, @c, @dist) = {
      match (dist > 0) and Onboard(from, dir) {
        true  => {
          new here in {
            here!(Step(from, dir)) |
            for (@s <- here) {
              for (@v <<- @[*b,"occ",s]) {          // PEEK, never consume
                  @[*b,"atk",s,c,p]!(("bears", from))
                | match v {
                    Nil => { Ray!(*b, s, dir, p, c, dist - 1) }  // ray continues
                    _   => { Nil }                              // blocked here
                  }
              }
            }
          }
        }
        false => { Nil }
      }
    }

  // Every occupied square emits its piece's rays; then the kings emit theirs
  // to deposit pins. Rem 5.4: this terminates and is confluent.
  | contract Settle(b) = {
      new each in {
          Squares!(*b, *each)
        | for (@s <= each) {
            for (@v <<- @[*b,"occ",s]) {
              match v {
                Nil => { Nil }
                _   => { Directions!(v, s, *b) }   // per-type ray emission
              }
            }
          }
        | Pins!(*b)
      }
    }

  // A pin marker is deposited by walking OUT from a king through the first own
  // piece to the first enemy slider on the same ray. Pin-awareness is
  // therefore PERCEPTION: a piece does not reason about whether it is pinned,
  // it reads whether a marker stands on its square. This is the note's best
  // instance of read-versus-drive (Sec. 5.3).
  | contract Pins(b) = {
      new k in {
          Kings!(*b, *k)
        | for (@(ksq, c) <= k) {
            new d in {
                Rays8!(*d)
              | for (@dir <= d) { PinWalk!(*b, ksq, dir, c, Nil) }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  A PLY IS A JOIN  --  Sec. 6, Def. 6.1 and Prop. 6.2.
  //
  //  One N-ary join over the occupancy cells the move touches, guarded on the
  //  constraint family, re-emitting every consumed cell with its post-move
  //  payload. A failed guard leaves the messages in the tuple space, which is
  //  the platform's own semantics for a failed `where`, so a refusal is a
  //  no-op and not a rollback.
  //
  //  ARITY IS THE MOVE TAXONOMY:
  //     2  quiet move        (*p, Nil)        |-> (Nil, *p)
  //     2  capture           (*p, *q)         |-> (Nil, *p), q terminated
  //     3  en passant        (*p, Nil, *q)    |-> (Nil, *p, Nil), q terminated
  //     4  castling          (*k,Nil,Nil,*r)  |-> (Nil, *k, *r, Nil)
  //     2  promotion         (*p, .)          |-> (Nil, *p'), p retired
  //
  //  This is what keeps the redex positions of the ply rule in bijection with
  //  the legal moves, and hence what keeps <K_m> a GENERATED modality rather
  //  than a derived abbreviation (Cor. 6.3).
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Ply(b, @m, ret) = {
      match Kind(m) {

        // --- quiet move: arity 2 -------------------------------------------
        "quiet" => {
          for ( @vf <- @[*b,"occ",From(m)] & @vt <- @[*b,"occ",To(m)]
                where Legal(m, vf, vt) ) {                        //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",From(m)]!(Nil)
            | @[*b,"occ",To(m)]!(vf)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // --- capture: arity 2, and the occupant is EATEN --------------------
        //  Harvest routes the captured agent's metabolic name to the capturer.
        //  The arena mints nothing; predation is legal here for precisely one
        //  reason, and it is that somebody took a piece.
        "capture" => {
          for ( @vf <- @[*b,"occ",From(m)] & @vt <- @[*b,"occ",To(m)]
                where Legal(m, vf, vt) ) {                        //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",From(m)]!(Nil)
            | @[*b,"occ",To(m)]!(vf)
            | Harvest!(Stack(vt), Stack(vf), *display)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // --- castling: arity 4, all-or-nothing ------------------------------
        //  The same shape as the four-cell SWAP of the cost-accounting demo:
        //  the guard decides BEFORE any leg fires, so no interleaving can
        //  leave the rook moved and the king not.
        "castle" => {
          for ( @vk <- @[*b,"occ",KFrom(m)] & @v1 <- @[*b,"occ",KTo(m)]
              & @v2 <- @[*b,"occ",RTo(m)]   & @vr <- @[*b,"occ",RFrom(m)]
                where CastleLegal(m, vk, v1, v2, vr) ) {          //! ideal
              @[*b,"occ",KFrom(m)]!(Nil) | @[*b,"occ",KTo(m)]!(vk)
            | @[*b,"occ",RFrom(m)]!(Nil) | @[*b,"occ",RTo(m)]!(vr)
            | Settle!(*b) | ret!(("played", m))
          }
        }

        // en passant (arity 3) and promotion follow the same pattern.
        _ => { ret!(("refused", m)) }
      }
    }

  // Conservation: nothing minted, the loser's stack MOVED into the winner's.
  | contract Harvest(mLost, mWon, ret) = {
      for (@a <- mLost & @c <- mWon) { mWon!(a + c) | ret!(("ate", a)) }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE FRANCHISE  --  Sec. 7.4. One LINEAR advocacy token per live piece per
  //  round, into a located stack whose channel is manufactured from the
  //  piece's identity. This is the polity listing's pre-election phase
  //  verbatim: a persistent claim on the roll metered to one linear token per
  //  epoch. It is a SOURCE keyed to identity, not a mint.
  //
  //  It is also what makes Thm. 7.6 arithmetic rather than statistical.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Franchise(@ids, @r) = {
      match ids {
        [] => { Nil }
        [id ...rest] => {
            @[*player,"V",id,r]!(("vote", id, r))
          | Franchise!(rest, r)
        }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  A PIECE  --  Sec. 11.3, steps 4-6 of the turn protocol.
  //
  //  A piece enumerates ITS OWN moves from ITS OWN square. Nobody computes the
  //  union of candidates; the union is what the advocacy namespace IS.
  //  Evaluation is an ASSAY: the rung sits in guard position, so evaluating is
  //  testing and confirmation is the rendezvous. By Prop. 17 of the scientist
  //  note the number of candidates a piece can afford to evaluate is its stack
  //  divided by the price of one check, so a poor piece does not survey its
  //  own branching.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Piece(b, @id, @rung, @r, m) = {
      new cand, best in {
          Candidates!(*b, id, *cand)
        | for (@c <- cand where Sat(c, rung, id)) {               //! ideal
            best!(c)
          }
        | for (@c <- best) { Advocate!(id, c, r) }
      }
    }

  // Spending the token IS the advocacy. A candidate move c is a term, so @c is
  // a name; the advocacy channel is manufactured from it. An OPEN in the
  // coalition sense is the set of pieces that have advocated for a given move
  // this round -- described by a name predicate, never enumerated.
  | contract Advocate(@id, @c, @r) = {
      for (@tok <- @[*player,"V",id,r]) {          // LINEAR: spent once
        @[*player,"adv",@c]!(id)
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE COMMIT GATE  --  Sec. 7.5, Def. 7.5.
  //
  //  QUORUM IS A JOIN ARITY. The gate is a k-ary join on one advocacy channel;
  //  it fires exactly when k distinct advocacy sends stand there, and it
  //  CONSUMES them. Shown for k = 3; the general case is generated.
  //
  //  THM 7.6. Two gates on distinct candidates consume 2k tokens drawn from a
  //  pool of S linear tokens, so two quorums require S >= 2k. With S = 16 and
  //  k >= 9 a SPLIT IS STRUCTURALLY IMPOSSIBLE, for any policies whatever, and
  //  no cross-inhibition machinery is needed to secure it. Safety is free;
  //  liveness is not, and that is what Round is for.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Gate(@c, @k, ret) = {
      for ( @a1 <- @[*player,"adv",@c]
          & @a2 <- @[*player,"adv",@c]
          & @a3 <- @[*player,"adv",@c] ) {
        @[*player,"commit"]!(@c) | ret!(("quorum", @c, [a1,a2,a3]))
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE ROUND  --  Sec. 7.6, Def. 7.8 and Def. 7.9.
  //
  //  Unconsumed advocacy is LEFT STANDING and is readable, so a piece may
  //  re-advocate having PERCEIVED where the rest of the society stood. That is
  //  the bees reading dance strength at the cluster: cheap perceptual price for
  //  the reversible decision, expensive interaction price for the irreversible
  //  one. Cross-inhibition is deliberately absent -- it is one more mechanism
  //  and the swarm measurement recorded that it suppresses CORRECT advocacy
  //  too. Deadlock is broken by re-advocacy and, failing that, by the clock.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Round(b, @ids, @r, @maxR, ret) = {
      new fired in {
          Franchise!(ids, r)
        | Pieces!(*b, ids, r)
        | Gates!(ids, r, *fired)
        | for (@res <- fired) { ret!(res) }
        | for (@t <- Timeout(r)) {                                //! ideal
            match r < maxR {
              true  => { Round!(*b, ids, r + 1, maxR, *ret) }
              // Resolver of last resort: the CLOCK, not a piece. It holds no
              // evaluation function and no preferences; its firing rate is a
              // measurement (M4), because a society that reaches it often is a
              // society whose quorum parameters are wrong.
              false => { MostAdvocated!(ids, r, *ret) }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE LADDER  --  Sec. 11.4. Cumulative, highest priority first. The rung
  //  index is the modal depth of the conjunct it adds, hence the ultrametric
  //  stage: d(Phi_r, Phi_{r+1}) = 2^-r. Prices are kappa(d) = 2^d per
  //  candidate examined, charged by the depth of the formula the rung installs.
  //
  //  Note where the alternation is. Rungs 1, 2 and 5 have no modality at all:
  //  capturing, not hanging, and centralising are PERCEPTION, reading the
  //  markers the last settling deposited. The one genuinely alternating rung
  //  is 4, a box under a diamond, which by Prop. 6 of the scientist note has
  //  no finite confirming witness. In noughts that rung was also, measurably,
  //  the worst purchase on the ladder. Whether that repeats in chess is M2,
  //  and it is the whole reason for the project.
  // ==================================================================

  //! ideal
  //  Emp(s)        := out(occ(s))@Nil
  //  Bears(p,s)    := exists c. out(atk(s,c,p)) TOP
  //  Fork(p)       := Threat(p) |_0^{N_atk} Threat(p)
  //  Bearing(s)    := <E>_{N_pl}^V  lambda p. Bears(p,s) (x) Available(p)
  //  <<K_m>>phi    := the ply join at m fires, the board settles, phi holds
  | contract Ladder(@r, ret) = {
      match r {
        0 => { ret!( @{ TOP } ) }
        1 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m)) } ) }
        2 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m) } ) }
        3 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me) } ) }
        4 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them) } ) }
        5 => { ret!( @{ exists m. Capture(m) and not Defended(to(m))
                                  or not Hangs(m)
                                  or <<K m>> Fork(me)
                                  or forall m2. <<K m>>[K m2] not Fork(them)
                                  or Central(to(m)) or Develops(m) } ) }
        // The coalition rung. Over a quorum algebra (x) = min this is attained,
        // so Check returns the WITNESS coalition and not merely its value: the
        // society commits to a move TOGETHER WITH the pieces that justify it.
        6 => { ret!( @{ exists m. exists s. <<K m>> (Bearing(s) >= v) } ) }
        // chi is listed for completeness. Unlike noughts it is NOT exact at any
        // finite unfolding, and by Rem. 3.1 that is exactly the condition under
        // which the ecology does not die of its own perfection.
        7 => { ret!( @{ nu X. forall m. [K m](not Lost
                                and exists m2. <<K m2>> X) } ) }
      }
    }

  // Revision walks the lattice. The move has the two parameters the metric
  // grades: a LOCUS and a DIRECTION. Refutation rate is the temperature, and it
  // is an observable the assays already emit, not a schedule imposed from
  // outside. NB no case touches the piece's ADVOCACY STYLE: that is the
  // stage-1 approximant, a move of distance 2^-1, and an individual can
  // neither reach it by deepening nor afford it if it could. It changes across
  // generations, by crossover, and that is where a model-backed mutation
  // operator earns its place (Sec. 10.2).
  | contract Revise(@rung, @refutes, @confirms, ret) = {
      match refutes * 4 > confirms {
        true  => { ret!(rung + 1) }
        false => { ret!(rung) }
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE CHECKER  --  Sec. 11.6. An instrument the player HOLDS, sited in the
  //  world so that sixteen agents share one cache. It receives only on the
  //  deposited families and NEVER on occ: otherwise the pride would move the
  //  zebra by looking at it. Verdicts are Kleene {f < b < t} with b carrying
  //  both readings -- the environment is stuck, or the observer could not
  //  afford to decide -- and the caller must not be able to tell which.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Check(@formula, @posHandle, @algebra, payer, ret) = {
      new r in {
          Live!(*payer, Price(formula), *r)
        | for (_ <- r) {
            Evaluate!(formula, posHandle, algebra, *ret)
            // ret!((verdict, value, witness, cost))
          }
        // If Live cannot fund the query the piece receives nothing, which the
        // protocol reads as verdict "b". Two bottoms, one value, by design.
      }
    }

  // ==================================================================
  //  THE ARENA  --  the rules, holding no stack of its own. It mints nothing;
  //  it ROUTES. An exogenous reward would need a referee with a mint, and a
  //  referee with a mint destroys conservation.
  //
  //  It is also where the one unavoidable universal lives. Check, pin and
  //  discovered check constrain the legality of EVERY piece's move; no piece
  //  can establish that locally and no quorum can establish it either, because
  //  an illegal move is not a move and a vote does not make it one. So the
  //  world refuses, and the player's own hypothesis language stays existential
  //  and finitely confirmable.
  // ==================================================================

  | contract Turn(b, @ids, @maxR, @who, over) = {
      new chosen in {
          Round!(*b, ids, 1, maxR, *chosen)
        | for (@res <- chosen) {
            new played in {
                Ply!(*b, Move(res), *played)
              | for (@out <- played) {
                  match out {
                    ("played", m) => {
                        Adjudicate!(*b, *over, who)
                      | Turn!(*b, Live_ids(ids), maxR, 3 - who, *over)
                    }
                    // A refused guard left the messages in the tuple space, so
                    // the position is untouched and the clock simply retries.
                    ("refused", m) => { Turn!(*b, ids, maxR, who, *over) }
                  }
                }
            }
          }
      }
    }

  //! ideal -- mate, stalemate and the draw conditions are modal formulae over
  //  the deposited families, not scans of a list. The fifty-move and repetition
  //  rules are the only genuinely HISTORICAL facts in chess and nothing here
  //  uses the history monad; that is open problem Q4.
  | contract Adjudicate(b, over, @who) = {
      new r in {
          @[*b,"state"]!(*r)
        | for (@pos <- r where pos |= @{ Mate(3 - who) }) {
            over!(("won", who))
          }
        | for (@pos <- r where pos |= @{ Stalemate(3 - who) }) {
            over!("draw")
          }
      }
    }

  | Boot!(Nil)
}
